+
+**Conclusion.**
+
+- **The timing signal held up.** In all four runs the selected model was balanced between long and short. Timing-only Sharpe ranged from 1.9 to 2.6, and test Sharpe from 1.4 to 2.6, with a mean of about 2.0.
+- **It beat always-short in three of four runs.**
+- **The winning objective depends on the seed.** One seed selected plain `MSE`, so the evidence that the economic losses cause the edge is inconclusive.
+- **The edge does not survive costs.** The selected models trade about 1.5–2.2 units of the pair per day, and their break-even cost is about 5–6 bps per unit traded. At an assumed 5 bps, net Sharpe is roughly zero. Trading less often, for example only when P(up) is far from 50%, is the obvious next step.
+
+ForGAN's test Sharpe across seeds 1–3 ranged from −0.91 to 0.93.
